    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5b63c087_7c7b5d03",
        "filename": "board/treeya/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 223,
      "author": {
        "id": 1104704
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-01-06T20:23:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Dynamic probing is not right, as i2c_xfer will timeout and impact the boot time.\nWhy can we use set the sensors based on the sku id?",
      "revId": "8fc98f0ba30c87c702381862562430e29e686632",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "33893e30_e452c8be",
        "filename": "board/treeya/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 223,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-01-07T02:03:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I added CPRINTS() before and after i2c_xfer_unlocked(), and executed 3 times per 2 sensor addresses on treeya board. The first time is about 1.6ms, and the reset times are about 500 us per time. It looks like if sensor numbers is X, the latency time is 1.6ms + 0.5ms*(X-1) in the worst case. Actually I only tested on treeya board, not sure other boards and EC chips.\nIt will enlarge sku ids. For example, the project already has 4 sku id, we have to add another 4 sku id if adding a new sensor. We want to konw if there is a good way to fix it.",
      "parentUuid": "5b63c087_7c7b5d03",
      "revId": "8fc98f0ba30c87c702381862562430e29e686632",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cc23b023_852469e7",
        "filename": "board/treeya/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 223,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-01-07T07:31:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Seems it\u0027s fine for treeya board at least. The i2c irq will occur if the address is not right on the ninth clock of the i2c transmission.\nNEGACK(Negative Acknowledge). Set by hardware when a transmission is not acknowledged on the ninth clock(in this case SDAST is not set to 1). When NEGACK is set, the SMBus is stalled.\nhttps://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/refs/heads/master/chip/npcx/i2c.c#779",
      "parentUuid": "33893e30_e452c8be",
      "revId": "8fc98f0ba30c87c702381862562430e29e686632",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "51d485c7_7856bb91",
        "filename": "board/treeya/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 223,
      "author": {
        "id": 1104704
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-01-10T08:21:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "8 sku looks safer and more deterministic: you could use a bit in the skuid to determine which sensors you are using.\nThis code assumes the sensor has \"booted\" when EC is running. \n- That may not be true depending on the power domain. (from .active_mask, it seems the sensors are powered off in S5).\n- If the sensor is a little slow (see driver/accel_kionix.c init() function).",
      "parentUuid": "cc23b023_852469e7",
      "revId": "8fc98f0ba30c87c702381862562430e29e686632",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "474dccbe_ac88f43f",
        "filename": "board/treeya/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 223,
      "author": {
        "id": 1176029
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-01-14T03:18:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "8 sku ids are just the example and\n    - we could end up to have more sku ids initially (ex: 8)\n    - we might end up to support more then two set sensors as well (ex: 3)\nThen finally we will have 24 sku ids.\n\nAnd partners is inputing the comment like judging the SKU ID in RMA center without factory server and BOM database is hard for them; as a result with to not increate the complexity of determining the SKU ID.",
      "parentUuid": "51d485c7_7856bb91",
      "revId": "8fc98f0ba30c87c702381862562430e29e686632",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e6caaef6_952e414b",
        "filename": "board/treeya/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 223,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-01-14T10:53:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That may not be true depending on the power domain. (from .active_mask, it seems the sensors are powered off in S5).\n\u003e\u003e\u003eThat\u0027s why sensor probing is only executed in RW stage. It is in S0 state in RW stage when HOOK_INIT is executed. Must the power state be S0 when execute HOOK_INIT in RW stage? If not, that would be a problem.\n\nIf the sensor is a little slow (see driver/accel_kionix.c init() function).\n\u003e\u003e\u003eMaybe the impact is little. And comparing with current code,sensor probing is much later. Maybe more than 2 or 3 seconds delays. One is S3 power state in RO, the other is HOOK INIT in RW. \nFor future, adding a probe function in every sensor driver operation struct may be a more better choice. Not only for some sensors init(), but also for probing sensors :)",
      "parentUuid": "474dccbe_ac88f43f",
      "revId": "8fc98f0ba30c87c702381862562430e29e686632",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "aafce4a3_075db2bb",
        "filename": "board/treeya/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 223,
      "author": {
        "id": 1104704
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-01-15T01:37:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e That may not be true depending on the power domain. (from .active_mask, it seems the sensors are powered off in S5).\n\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003eThat\u0027s why sensor probing is only executed in RW stage. It is in S0 state in RW stage when HOOK_INIT is executed. Must the power state be S0 when execute HOOK_INIT in RW stage? If not, that would be a problem.\nI am concern when the EC hibernates while in S5. If it resumes while in S5, the sensor may not be working.\n\u003e \n\u003e If the sensor is a little slow (see driver/accel_kionix.c init() function).\n\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003eMaybe the impact is little. And comparing with current code,sensor probing is much later. Maybe more than 2 or 3 seconds delays. One is S3 power state in RO, the other is HOOK INIT in RW. \n\u003e For future, adding a probe function in every sensor driver operation struct may be a more better choice. Not only for some sensors init(), but also for probing sensors :)",
      "parentUuid": "e6caaef6_952e414b",
      "revId": "8fc98f0ba30c87c702381862562430e29e686632",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5008fb1d_ac555d3c",
        "filename": "board/treeya/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 223,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-01-15T04:19:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You mean in S5 when running in EC RW stage，then enter hibernate? I test a coral board and a treeya board, EC would be as the same as EC power up. That means running RO first, then RW. This   function is indeed not controlled by active_mask, so I choose HOOK INIT and RW stage the two conditions to make sure the sensor can be probed sucessfully( might fail if not S0 at this moment, but i have no idea of similar scenario). As my suggestio above, maybe the better way to probe sensor is implement sensor_probe() in every sensor driver operation struct like sensor init().",
      "parentUuid": "aafce4a3_075db2bb",
      "revId": "8fc98f0ba30c87c702381862562430e29e686632",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
